So, I hate monks. I've only ascended one ever, and while it was
weaponless, it wasn't vegetarian and it wasn't armorless and really I
didn't enjoy it.  So a week or two ago when I realized I needed only a
human for a June hat trick --- and a consecutive neutral for a double
top with bells --- I was miffed that everyone I asked suggested a monk
as the most survivable class for a neutral human. I really wanted to just
play another wizard, but I was eventually shamed into starting a monk.

(note: if you follow along with the ttyrecs, I'm sure I'm messing up
the ordering here somewhat, but I really don't have time to fact-check
the order of operations on all of these things, sorry. If you do want 
to watch, I suggest starting with me going to try to take Kaen; from 
there it pretty much stays exciting, I think.)

As an exercise, I kept ten conducts down to Minetown, thinking it would
be good practice for some other conduct ascension attempt. I kept them
through Soko too, but kept price-IDing all the scrolls and spellbooks
on the theory that I would be using them soon. When I got the bag of
holding in Sokoban, I was pretty much committed to breaking the conducts,
and then on DL 7 or 8 (it was a really shallow Soko), I found bones.

They would have made me much happier if I hadn't been a conduct character
or hadn't been a monk: tons of scrolls, unholy water, a magic marker, a
bunch of spellbooks, a bunch of daggers. Oh, and SDSM. And wait, was that a
quarterstaff? So that cloak must be...

I got just a touch giddy. MR! Reflection! I put them on. Who cares if
they are cursed? I can enchant them up, I can dip them in fountains
since they'll make me able to survive the effects, I can wean myself
off of the armor when I get the shield of reflection from medusa, or I
can just wish one up at the Castle... 

Or, if I dare, I can take a Monk on a streak who started with Protection
(not Sleep, which would actually have been helpful), wear body armor all
game, and try to keep 10 conducts. 

I kind of knew that if I didn't try for it now, I probably wouldn't ever
try for it again.  With a wand of fire in open inventory I could always
break illit to survive if I got too bogged down, and all of my scrolls
were price-ID'd if I wanted to go that route. Minetown was even coaligned.
At this point I couldn't hit anything, but I started kicking them, and that
worked pretty well; it only had 50% effectivenesss or so, but thanks to 
some +5 iron shoes I found on a dwarf in the Mines, my AC was -10 or so, 
and that was more than enough to make killing things slowly OK.

I descended for the luckstone with little incident, irritatingly not
finding any helpful tools along the way. I grabbed some things that
engraved instead of wrote 'x' to throw at a coaligned unicorn to get my
luck up; I usually just sacrifice, but that wasn't going to fly here.
(Eventually I found a blessed touchstone, which simplified matters
significantly.)  In the meantime I descended slowly, pacifying tameables
as I went.

I found that some monsters were harder to kill than others, and
those monsters got +0 daggers from afar. It turns out that they were
thick-skinned, and the only reason I was able to kick them at all was
that my +5 iron boots got their enchantment bonus to damage. ... minus,
of course, a penalty for the corrosion and rust they quickly picked up.
The descent to Medusa passed largely without incident; I found +0 jumping
boots but didn't wear them. I forgot about them when I got to Medusa,
in fact, and after going back up to try to polytrap some good pets,
I said "To hell with it" and dug down. I had an instrument! What more 
did I need?

I quickly learned that:

 * most pets are useless, even jabberwocks will get themselves stupidly
   killed when you're not paying attention
 * minotaurs hit hard, burning teleportation charges you really wanted to 
   save for the endgame
 * winged gargoyles also hit hard, but are dumber about following you
   (I'm not sure this is true, but it sure seemed that way)

I ended up making it to the Castle, though without any pets, and
drawbridging everything but the two barracks. One was closed and I left it
closed; the other was not, and I killed all the soldiers and used a nearby
warhorse or something to pet-test the armor. +3 small shield! +1 helmet!
_sweet_. I also picked up the wand and put it in my bag; I learned after
my game concluded that it was 0:1. Oh well, if it wasn't for 0:1 wands,
I wouldn't have no wands at all...

I went back upstairs, reflected Medusa, and then got both a shield
and lev boots from the Perseus statue. Oh, I'm forgetting an important
detail here; at the time, I was wearing a ring of invisibility rather
than zapping myself with a wand of make invisible. This meant I could
petrify Medusa and ID things in shops. After Medusa I ended up just using
the wand, which gave me more ring freedom to use the slow digestion and
free action I think I had by that point.

Gehennom was miserable. I couldn't kill half the monsters, and kept having
to go back up to make new pet dragons or umber hulks or master liches only
to bring them all down and have them die doing something absurdly dumb.
Summoners are horrible and I had a lot of close calls. At this point,
mind you, I was XL 14 or 15 (I got 15 by XP) and had alchemized what little
I could for like 80-odd HP. I had Con 18 pretty early, too; stupid monks.
Eventually I got to Vlad and at that point had 100 or so HP.

I had to burn my last or second-to-last potion of full healing on Vlad.
That's right, Vlad. He nearly destroyed me. A bunch of people were
watching and had made fun of me for being afraid of Vlad; at the end
they all agreed it was totally justified because damn, if you're AC -12
and HP 100 and you do like 5 damage a turn, that's _dangerous_. Sadly
he's poison resistant; I had discovered that a lot of big baddies, like
flayers and winged gargoyles and I think minotaurs, can fall to a few
poison darts without too much trouble. But this gave me the Candelabrum 
and an amulet of lifesaving, which made me way less nervous about life.

I went back up to ID rings and get holy water, and it turned out that
I had conflict. (Oh, I also got regeneration pretty early on; it was
a good ring to wear when getting swarmed on DL 10-20.) With levitation
boots, I had some sort of pathetic excuse for a full kit. With conflict
I realized that I might actually ascend. I dragged my current menagerie
of ponies or whatever to the one polytrap I had found in the main dungeon
and... I ended up with a purple worm.

My vision went all red and focused. I had spent a couple of hours planning
the different strategies I might use to handle Kaen. I had come up with 
the following list:

 * Wand of death while still meditating
 * Sleep
 * Paralysis and free action
 * Levitate over the lava and then use attack wands
 * Absurd pets and conflict
 * Purple worm and conflict
 * Die

I figured I wouldn't get another purple worm out of the polytrap, or at
least that I didn't have the patience to. I had already explored the first
four levels of Home for loot and XP, so I just had to dart down with the
worm. I figured that even if I did get a wand of death, I'd rather have
the worm, and so I went down to Home 5, which was ... a Kaen bonesfile.

newerp, if you are reading this, your attempt to kill Kaen with the AC
that armor must have given you and all of your potions of healing, extra
healing, and full healing still in your bag was insane. However, all of
your potions of healing were in your bag, and that probably saved my life.
I alchemized to 200HP and, after taking a deep breath, went downstairs.

Kaen woke up. Of course. I wasn't able to get to the lava. Of course.
Dude does like 50 damage per turn when you have AC -7, it's insane. I
managed to get up the stairs with my worm and without him (yay speed
system!), heal up, and then brought him back up. I missed the first time
with my wand of sleep, but the second time it worked, and Pinwheel the 
purple worm ate Kaen. AWESOME. There was much cheering. At this point I
felt that the only real challenges left were the Sanctum and Astral.

I was a bit wrong; when the purple worm died on DL44, Gehennom got hard
again; I have respect for liches again, which had faded in a string of
games where I just stomped through the game. Also, there was no cockatrice
in the wizard's tower, so I had to conflict my way up and then enter,
which led to me being surrounded by summons on the same turn as a giant
eel swung itself around me. The summons, of course, included a master
mind flayer.  I was forced to apply a wand of teleportation. The next 
summon storm included a purple worm, who graciously ate Rodney.

I actually forgot the Book at first :) Once I went back for it, the
VS level was the next level down, and I had to uncurse my Bell and
Candelabrum, which left me with like two holy water. I went downstairs
and of course there was a &%&^%*& arch-lich, but I dropped scare monster
and used conflict. I snuck through the graveyard mostly unharmed and then
used conflict and jumping to get through the priests; the high priest
fell to the first death ray, thank _god_. (Is he poison resistant? That
was my backup plan, inasmuch as I had one.) I had picked up a cursed 
potion of gain level, and used that to get out...

The mysterious force was reasonably kind. That was good, because I was 
down to one holy water when I got back up to my wand of cancellation on
DL 7 or so. I ended up taking 6 or 7 up into the planes with me. I had a 
glass orb, but I thought it might have been cursed, so I cancelled it
to make it uncursed. I felt smart about this until I realized it was now
chargeless. Oops.

EARTH:

The portal was way the hell to the right, but that wasn't a big deal.
I didn't bother jumping to conserve food, since I had failed to loot 
the castle for it on the way up. Whoops.

AIR:

Invis + lev + conflict + stealth = <3 I was engulfed by _one_ air
elemental. _One_.

I had been flayed, so I didn't know slow monster from polymorph, but it
turns out that zapping either one inside an air elemental will get you
out, so it didn't matter! I thought I didn't have secret door detection,
so waited for something to find me the portal.

FIRE:

An Archon spawned on the right, but once I was on the left. The wielded
amulet got me through this one. The plane of Fire isn't even hard with 
an AC -7 ten-conduct monk: Conflict is broken, if anyone with the power
to fix this is listening. (and I will so get shot for saying that) 

WATER:

Ho hum, ho hum, oh hey check it a portal.

ASTRAL:

OH MY GOD. So I started off by jumping left. It turned out that was Death;
I tried to get away but was surrounded by purple Ls. Not by summons,
but by the liches themselves. Oh God. Death drained some max HP because
I could get nowhere and could not hit him. (Had I not worn the armor, I
would have been AC 4.) A conflicted minotaur eventually killed Death and
I was able to jump past to the altar --- which was Chaotic. I grabbed a
cockatrice corpse and took out the priests and angels, but then death was
coming, so I led him around the high temple. I headed up to the center
after whacking another purple L who showed up, and that was Pestilence,
who I avoided.  At this point I was pretty sure I was out of teleport
charges (turned out I had: 1).  I also figured I was out of death charges
(actually had: 2). Of course, the center altar was lawful.

I was actually starting to feel nervous at this point. I took a look at
my inventory and at the map. I considered a bunch of stupid things. Then
I realized that I had three amulets of lifesaving. ... Oh. I jumped past
Famine to get to the third altar, which of course was mine, sacrificed
the Amulet to the god I had so long ignored, and then proceeded to take
a four-hour nap (which is why I am still up now writing this).

The entire game I never _wielded_ a weapon for fear of misusing it. I 
never stepped on an altar square except that high altar, and was afraid 
to whistle a pet onto an altar in case they might drop an item. Until I
had MR I was searching three times every square below DL7. This game was
insane. But, I did it. Woo!

Apparently it's the same conduct set as Marvin and Tenaya's 10-conduct
ascensions; eit_brad did foodless but not atheist. Are there other
10-conduct games out there? Anyone manage 11 yet? :)

Thanks for reading; there are comments in the game dump, too. I'm
happy to answer any questions about strategy or whatever.

